<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rio Protocol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Protocol of Peace, Friendship and Boundaries, Rio de Janeiro Protocol · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuadorian-peruvian-war">Ecuadorian–Peruvian War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> Protocol was signed on 29 January 1942 by Ecuador and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> after Peru's victory in the war of 1941, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, Chile and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as guarantors. It assigned most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> territory claimed by Ecuador to Peru. Ecuador later declared it unworkable, and border clashes recurred until the final settlement of 1998.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rio-protocol.jpg" alt="Rio Protocol" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reproducción fotográfica · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Protocolo_de_R%C3%ADo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
